  "knowledgePattern": "Translate the interval and separate the added signed area",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Check the input at each end",
      "body": "<p>For each option, put the lower and upper limits into the expression inside <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>. Compare those two values with 1 and 5.</p>",
      "recap": "A horizontal shift moves the graph and its limits together without changing area.",
      "pitfall": "Use the whole input of f, including its plus or minus sign.",
      "pause": "Mark which options cover the same part of f as the original integral.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b3-l08",
          "reminder": "A translation preserves area when the interval moves with the graph."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Check the extra term separately",
      "body": "<p>Ignore f for a moment. In each remaining option, work out whether the added polynomial has zero integral over the stated limits.</p>",
      "recap": "Opposite heights on opposite sides of zero cancel in a signed integral.",
      "pitfall": "Symmetric limits alone do not make every integral zero.",
      "pause": "Combine your endpoint check and extra-term check to choose an option.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p2-b3-l04",
          "reminder": "Compare the graph’s heights at x and −x when the limits are symmetric."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> The graph of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the graph of f moved left by 3. Its integral from −2 to 2 therefore equals the original integral from 1 to 5.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> The added <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math> has opposite heights at opposite inputs, so its signed integral over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is zero. Consequently <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x0222B;</mo><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math>, giving B.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> To rule out the others as universal identities, take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for A, D and F: their added square has positive integral. For C and E take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>; their shifted intervals differ from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and their integrals are not I. A special choice that happens to work would not prove an identity for every continuous f.</p>"
}
